-- `l4 run` and `l4 batch` name a different number of failures here, and both
-- exit 0. #817's memo keys an argument that is not yet evaluated by where it
-- is, and one that is by its value. Under `l4 run`, `#EVAL k` evaluates `k`
-- first, so `r 5` and `r k` share a key and Alice's miss is named once;
-- `l4 batch` drops `#EVAL` directives, so `k` is still unevaluated when the
-- memo keys it and the miss is named twice. unstable names it twice under
-- both. This is #817's behaviour, made visible by the blame list, not the
-- port's (the adversarial pass of 2026-11-20; NOTE [The same breach on both
-- sides is named once] in L4.EvaluateLazy.Machine).
DECLARE Person IS ONE OF Alice
DECLARE Action IS ONE OF deliver

GIVEN d IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC Person Action
r d MEANS PARTY Alice MUST deliver WITHIN d

GIVEN x IS A DEONTIC Person Action
      y IS A DEONTIC Person Action
GIVETH A DEONTIC Person Action
both x y MEANS x RAND y

k MEANS 2 + 3
c MEANS both (r 5) (r k)

#EVAL k

#TRACE c AT 0 WITH
  WAITUNTIL 20

@export default trivial entry point, so l4 batch has a function to call
zzT MEANS TRUE
